problems/01-unknown-equality.md
     1	# P01. Unknown equality
     2	
     3	**Probes:** how a system represents a relation between unknowns before
     4	either has a value, and whether a later value reaches all of them.
     5	
     6	## Vocabulary
     7	
     8	conc(a, b, c): c is a followed by b. It is defined by:
     9	
    10	1. conc(⟨⟩, b, b), for every sequence b
    11	2. conc(⟨h⟩ ++ a, b, ⟨h⟩ ++ c) if conc(a, b, c)
    12	
    13	So conc relates sequences only.
    14	
    15	## P01a. A chain of equations
    16	
    17	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    18	|---|---|---|
    19	| 1 | x = y | every assignment with x = y; z free |
    20	| 2 | y = z | every assignment with x = y = z |
    21	| 3 | z = 2 | x = y = z = 2 |
    22	
    23	## P01b. An unknown inside a structure
    24	
    25	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    26	|---|---|---|
    27	| 1 | conc(⟨1,2⟩, s, t) | t = ⟨1,2⟩ ++ s, for every sequence s |
    28	| 2 | s = ⟨3⟩ | s = ⟨3⟩, t = ⟨1,2,3⟩ |
    29	
    30	## P01c. A self-referential equation
    31	
    32	| stage | information |
    33	|---|---|
    34	| 1 | x = ⟨1⟩ ++ x |
    35	
    36	Two readings:
    37	
    38	- **finite:** no finite sequence satisfies it. Valid outcome: none.
    39	- **rational:** over rational trees (finite or infinite terms with
    40	  finitely many distinct subterms), it has one solution, the infinite
    41	  sequence ⟨1, 1, 1, …⟩.
    42	
    43	A result is classified under each reading. A cyclic value, presented as
    44	such, is *complete* under the rational reading and *unsound* under the
    45	finite one.
    46	
    47	## Observations
    48	
    49	- After P01a stage 1 and P01b stage 1, how the relation between unknowns is
    50	  held: as one shared unknown, as a recorded equation, or otherwise.
    51	- Whether the value of P01a stage 3, or P01b stage 2, reaches every unknown
    52	  it determines, and in how many steps.
    53	- P01c: the report, and the configuration's occurs-check setting.
problems/02-late-information.md
     1	# P02. Late information
     2	
     3	**Probes:** whether relations that cannot yet be decided are kept and
     4	resumed when information arrives, and at what cost.
     5	
     6	From Elcock (1982, p. 123), who gives the three relations in this order.
     7	All unknowns are integers.
     8	
     9	## The relations
    10	
    11	- R1: u + v = 16
    12	- R2: w · u = v
    13	- R3: u + 10 = 12
    14	
    15	## P02a. In Elcock's order
    16	
    17	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    18	|---|---|---|
    19	| 1 | R1 | every integer pair with u + v = 16; w free (infinite) |
    20	| 2 | R2 | exactly ten triples (u, v, w), below |
    21	| 3 | R3 | u = 2, v = 14, w = 7 |
    22	
    23	After stage 2, u · (w + 1) = 16, so u divides 16. The ten triples are
    24	(1, 15, 15), (2, 14, 7), (4, 12, 3), (8, 8, 1), (16, 0, 0), (−1, 17, −17),
    25	(−2, 18, −9), (−4, 20, −5), (−8, 24, −3) and (−16, 32, −2).
    26	
    27	## P02b. Every order
    28	
    29	The three relations, one per stage, in all six orders. After each stage,
    30	the valid outcomes depend only on which relations have arrived:
    31	
    32	| relations so far | valid outcomes |
    33	|---|---|
    34	| R1 | u + v = 16, w free (infinite) |
    35	| R2 | v = w · u (infinite) |
    36	| R3 | u = 2; v and w free (infinite) |
    37	| R1, R2 | the ten triples above |
    38	| R1, R3 | u = 2, v = 14; w free (infinite) |
    39	| R2, R3 | u = 2, v = 2w, for every integer w (infinite) |
    40	| R1, R2, R3 | u = 2, v = 14, w = 7 |
    41	
    42	## P02c. All at once
    43	
    44	R1, R2 and R3 as one stage, in that order. Valid outcome: u = 2, v = 14,
    45	w = 7.
    46	
    47	## Observations
    48	
    49	- After each stage that does not decide everything: what is held for each
    50	  relation not yet decided, and what each is waiting for, in the system's
    51	  terms.
    52	- When the last relation arrives: in what order the held relations are
    53	  taken up, and how many times each is examined, from a trace if the
    54	  system gives one.
    55	- P02b: whether all six orders give the same final outcome, and the steps
    56	  of each.
    57	- Incremental cost, restated cost, restart cost and repeated work for each
    58	  stage (`METRICS.md`).
problems/03-reversible-relations.md
     1	# P03. Reversible relations
     2	
     3	**Probes:** whether one definition of a relation serves for every
     4	combination of known and unknown arguments, and whether the system knows
     5	when it has found every answer.
     6	
     7	## Vocabulary
     8	
     9	conc, as in P01, with its cases in that order.
    10	
    11	rev(a, b): b is a in reverse order. It is defined, in this order, by:
    12	
    13	1. rev(⟨⟩, ⟨⟩)
    14	2. rev(⟨h⟩ ++ l, r) if rev(l, q) and conc(q, ⟨h⟩, r), with the two
    15	   conjuncts in that order
    16	
    17	## Queries
    18	
    19	Each is one stage.
    20	
    21	| | query | valid outcomes |
    22	|---|---|---|
    23	| P03a | conc(⟨1,2⟩, ⟨3⟩, c) | c = ⟨1,2,3⟩ |
    24	| P03b | conc(a, b, ⟨1,2⟩) | three: (⟨⟩, ⟨1,2⟩), (⟨1⟩, ⟨2⟩), (⟨1,2⟩, ⟨⟩) |
    25	| P03c | conc(⟨1⟩, b, ⟨1,2,3⟩) | b = ⟨2,3⟩ |
    26	| P03d | conc(a, ⟨3⟩, ⟨1,2,3⟩) | a = ⟨1,2⟩ |
    27	| P03e | rev(⟨1,2,3⟩, b) | b = ⟨3,2,1⟩ |
    28	| P03f | rev(a, ⟨1,2,3⟩) | a = ⟨3,2,1⟩, exactly one |
    29	
    30	## Observations
    31	
    32	- Whether every valid outcome is reported, and in what order.
    33	- After the last valid outcome: whether the computation finishes, holds
    34	  alternatives pending, or goes on to the budget. P03f has exactly one
    35	  solution, so a system that reports it and then runs out of budget is
    36	  *complete* with *budget*.
    37	- Whether any direction needed reformulation, and how.
problems/04-unbounded-alternative.md
     1	# P04. Unbounded alternative
     2	
     3	**Probes:** whether an alternative with no finite derivation stops the
     4	system from finding one that has one, and how the order in which a
     5	recursive definition is written affects its use.
     6	
     7	## Vocabulary
     8	
     9	loop(x): defined only by
    10	
    11	1. loop(x) if loop(x)
    12	
    13	Read as the least relation, loop has no instances.
    14	
    15	alist(s): s is a finite sequence of the atom ᴀ. Two definitions are given.
    16	They have the same meaning and are written differently.
    17	
    18	- **alist-left**, in this order:
    19	  1. alist(s) if alist(m) and s = ⟨ᴀ⟩ ++ m, with the two conjuncts in that
    20	     order
    21	  2. alist(⟨⟩)
    22	- **alist-right**, in this order:
    23	  1. alist(⟨⟩)
    24	  2. alist(⟨ᴀ⟩ ++ m) if alist(m)
    25	
    26	## Queries
    27	
    28	Each is one stage.
    29	
    30	| | query | valid outcomes |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| P04a | loop(x) or x = 1, in that order | x = 1 |
    33	| P04b | x = 1 or loop(x), in that order | x = 1 |
    34	| P04c | alist(⟨ᴀ,ᴀ⟩), with alist-left | true: one solution, no unknowns |
    35	| P04d | alist(s), with alist-left | infinitely many: s = ⟨⟩, ⟨ᴀ⟩, ⟨ᴀ,ᴀ⟩, … |
    36	| P04e | alist(⟨ᴀ,ᴀ⟩), with alist-right | true |
    37	| P04f | alist(s), with alist-right | infinitely many, as P04d |
    38	
    39	## Observations
    40	
    41	- P04a and P04b: whether x = 1 is reported, and then whether the
    42	  computation finishes, holds alternatives pending, suspends or runs to
    43	  the budget.
    44	- P04c and P04e: the outcome, and whether the computation finishes.
    45	- P04d and P04f: which solutions are reported, in what order, and whether
    46	  every solution would be reached given enough steps. Compare only this
    47	  property across systems, not the number reported within the budget.
problems/05-late-contradiction.md
     1	# P05. Late contradiction
     2	
     3	**Probes:** how a system holds several alternatives while constraints that
     4	eliminate some of them arrive, and what happens to answers it has already
     5	given.
     6	
     7	## Vocabulary
     8	
     9	pick(x): a closed world of three facts, in this order: pick(1), pick(2),
    10	pick(3).
    11	
    12	x > 1 and x ≠ 2 are primitive comparisons.
    13	
    14	## P05a. Choice first
    15	
    16	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    17	|---|---|---|
    18	| 1 | pick(x) | x ∈ {1, 2, 3} |
    19	| 2 | x > 1 | x ∈ {2, 3} |
    20	| 3 | x ≠ 2 | x = 3 |
    21	
    22	## P05b. Constraint first
    23	
    24	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    25	|---|---|---|
    26	| 1 | x > 1 | every integer x > 1 |
    27	| 2 | pick(x) | x ∈ {2, 3} |
    28	
    29	## P05c. Contradiction
    30	
    31	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    32	|---|---|---|
    33	| 1 | pick(x) | x ∈ {1, 2, 3} |
    34	| 2 | x = 4 | none |
    35	| 3 | x = 3 | none |
    36	
    37	Stage 3 adds to stages that are already inconsistent, so the valid outcome
    38	stays empty.
    39	
    40	## Observations
    41	
    42	- After each stage: whether every surviving alternative is held at once, or
    43	  one is held and the others kept for backtracking, or the others are lost.
    44	- An answer reported at one stage and invalid at the next: how its
    45	  withdrawal is shown, if at all.
    46	- P05b stage 1: how a comparison on an unknown is treated: held, reported
    47	  as a residual, enumerated, or an error.
    48	- P05c: how the empty outcome is reported, and whether stage 3 can be
    49	  posed at all.
problems/06-negation.md
     1	# P06. Negation with unknowns
     2	
     3	**Probes:** whether negation gives valid outcomes when the unknowns it
     4	mentions are still unknown, and whether the order of information changes
     5	the outcome.
     6	
     7	not R holds of an assignment exactly when R does not hold of it. Definitions
     8	with negation are read under the stratified semantics
     9	(`SPECIFICATION.md`). Relations given by facts are in a closed world.
    10	
    11	## P06a. Negation, then a value
    12	
    13	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    14	|---|---|---|
    15	| 1 | not (x = 1) | every value x other than 1 |
    16	| 2 | x = 2 | x = 2 |
    17	
    18	## P06b. A value, then negation
    19	
    20	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    21	|---|---|---|
    22	| 1 | x = 2 | x = 2 |
    23	| 2 | not (x = 1) | x = 2 |
    24	
    25	## P06c. A contradiction through negation
    26	
    27	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    28	|---|---|---|
    29	| 1 | not (x = 1) | every value x other than 1 |
    30	| 2 | x = 1 | none |
    31	
    32	## P06d. Set difference
    33	
    34	mem(e, s): e is an element of the sequence s. It is defined, in this order,
    35	by:
    36	
    37	1. mem(e, ⟨e⟩ ++ t), for every sequence t
    38	2. mem(e, ⟨h⟩ ++ t) if mem(e, t)
    39	
    40	setdiff(s1, s2, d): d is the elements of s1 that are not elements of s2, in
    41	their order in s1. It is defined, in this order, by:
    42	
    43	1. setdiff(⟨⟩, s2, ⟨⟩)
    44	2. setdiff(⟨h⟩ ++ t, s2, d) if mem(h, s2) and setdiff(t, s2, d)
    45	3. setdiff(⟨h⟩ ++ t, s2, ⟨h⟩ ++ d) if not mem(h, s2) and setdiff(t, s2, d)
    46	
    47	| stage | information | valid outcomes |
    48	|---|---|---|
    49	| 1 | setdiff(⟨1,2,3,4⟩, ⟨2,4,6⟩, d) | d = ⟨1,3⟩ |
    50	
    51	A system with a set-forming construct may state the same definition with it.
    52	That counts as problem tokens too (`METRICS.md`, rule 6).
    53	
    54	## P06e. Clark's students
    55	
    56	A closed world, from Clark (1978) as rendered by Elcock (1990):
    57	
    58	- student(ʙʀᴏᴡɴ), student(ꜱᴍɪᴛʜ)
    59	- maths(ᴄ101), maths(ᴄ301)
    60	- takes(ʙʀᴏᴡɴ, ᴄ101), takes(ꜱᴍɪᴛʜ, ᴄ101), takes(ꜱᴍɪᴛʜ, ᴄ301)
    61	
    62	nonmajor(p): p is a student who does not take some maths course. It is
    63	defined by one case:
    64	
    65	1. nonmajor(p) if student(p) and maths(k) and not takes(p, k), with the
    66	   conjuncts in that order
    67	
    68	| | query | valid outcomes |
    69	|---|---|---|
    70	| P06e1 | nonmajor(ʙʀᴏᴡɴ) | true |
    71	| P06e2 | nonmajor(ꜱᴍɪᴛʜ) | false |
    72	| P06e3 | nonmajor(p) | p = ʙʀᴏᴡɴ |
    73	
    74	## Observations
    75	
    76	- Whether P06a and P06b give the same outcome. A system that reports no
    77	  solution for P06a is *unsound*; record the step at which the negation
    78	  was decided.
    79	- P06a stage 1: whether the negation is held as a condition, decided at
    80	  once, delayed, or an error. A representation that restricts x to integers
    81	  is *partial*.
    82	- P06e3: whether p and k are known before the negation is decided.
problems/07-simultaneous-constraints.md
     1	# P07. Simultaneous constraints
     2	
     3	**Probes:** which sets of arithmetic relations a system solves, which it
     4	leaves unresolved, and which it gets wrong.
     5	
     6	All unknowns are integers. Each set of relations is one stage, in the order
     7	given.
     8	
     9	| | relations | valid outcomes |
    10	|---|---|---|
    11	| P07a | x + y + z = 12, y + z = 7, z = 3 | x = 5, y = 4, z = 3 |
    12	| P07b | x + y = 10, x − y = 4 | x = 7, y = 3 |
    13	| P07c | x + y = 10 | every integer pair with x + y = 10 |
    14	| P07d | w · u = v, u = 2, v = 14 | w = 7 |
    15	| P07e | w · u = v, u = 2, v = 15 | none: 15 is not a multiple of 2 |
    16	| P07f | x + y = 10, x + y = 11 | none |
    17	| P07g | x · x = 9 | x = 3 or x = −3 |
    18	
    19	P07a is also posed in all six orders of its relations.
    20	
    21	## Observations
    22	
    23	- For each: the three classifications, with any residual recorded.
    24	- P07e and P07f: whether the inconsistency is detected. A system that holds
    25	  the relations unresolved is *complete* / *residual* / *inconsistency
    26	  undetected*, with *suspended*.
    27	- P07e: a solver over the rationals that reports w = 15/2 is *unsound*
    28	  under this problem's integers.
    29	- P07g: whether both solutions are found.
    30	- What mechanism solves each case that is solved: one relation at a time as
    31	  its values become known, propagation, elimination, or search.
problems/08-late-definition.md
     1	# P08. Late definition
     2	
     3	**Probes:** what an application of a relation that has no definition yet
     4	means to a system, and what happens when the definition arrives.
     5	
     6	rev and conc are as in P03.
     7	
     8	## What an undefined relation can mean
     9	
    10	A relation applied before its definition has arrived can be treated as:
    11	
    12	1. a relation that is false (it has no instances);
    13	2. a relation whose truth is unknown;
    14	3. a reference to a definition that is unavailable, which is an error;
    15	4. a computation awaiting its definition.
    16	
    17	These are different states, and a system may take any of them. Two readings
    18	fix the valid outcomes:
    19	
    20	- **closed:** an undefined relation has no instances (state 1);
    21	- **open:** the definition may yet arrive, so an application of it neither
    22	  holds nor fails, and the valid outcomes are those of the other
    23	  information alone.
    24	
    25	Neither reading is the valid one. A result records which of the four states
    26	the system takes, and is classified under both readings.
    27	
    28	Under the closed reading, a definition's arrival can withdraw an earlier
    29	valid conclusion (no solutions) although the information only grows.
    30	That is the non-monotonicity of conclusions that `SPECIFICATION.md`
    31	describes, and the withdrawal is recorded.
    32	
    33	## P08a. A definition that arrives
    34	
    35	| stage | information | closed reading | open reading |
    36	|---|---|---|---|
    37	| 1 | a = ⟨x, y, z⟩ | a = ⟨x, y, z⟩ | the same |
    38	| 2 | rev(a, b) | none | a = ⟨x, y, z⟩; b free; rev(a, b) pending |
    39	| 3 | x = y | none | x = y, a = ⟨y, y, z⟩; b free; pending |
    40	| 4 | the definitions of rev and conc | x = y, a = ⟨y, y, z⟩, b = ⟨z, y, y⟩ | the same |
    41	| 5 | y = z | x = y = z, a = b = ⟨z, z, z⟩ | the same |
    42	| 6 | b = ⟨42⟩ ++ c | x = y = z = 42, a = b = ⟨42, 42, 42⟩, c = ⟨42, 42⟩ | the same |
    43	
    44	Under the open reading, before the definition arrives:
    45	
    46	- a system that holds rev(a, b) as a pending application, and reports the
    47	  equations, is *complete* / *residual*;
    48	- a system that reports the equations and has silently dropped rev(a, b)
    49	  is also *complete*, with representation *solved form*, and the dropping
    50	  is recorded, since it will lose the application when the definition
    51	  comes;
    52	- a system that reports no solutions is *unsound* under the open reading,
    53	  and *complete* under the closed one.
    54	
    55	A declaration that changes how an undefined relation behaves (`:- dynamic`,
    56	the `unknown` flag) is control, and is listed (`METRICS.md`, rule 11).
    57	
    58	## P08b. A definition that never arrives
    59	
    60	Stages 1 to 3 of P08a, and no more. Record whether the system's report can
    61	be told apart from failure, and which of the four states it shows.
    62	
    63	## P08c. A definition that arrives in parts
    64	
    65	| stage | information | closed reading | open reading |
    66	|---|---|---|---|
    67	| 1 | the definition of conc | no unknowns | the same |
    68	| 2 | rev(⟨1, 2⟩, b) | none | b free; pending |
    69	| 3 | rev, case 1 only: rev(⟨⟩, ⟨⟩) | none: case 1 alone does not cover a sequence of two | b free; pending, since case 2 may yet arrive |
    70	| 4 | rev, case 2 | b = ⟨2, 1⟩ | the same |
    71	
    72	## Observations
    73	
    74	- Which of the four states the system takes, and how a pending application
    75	  is shown.
    76	- Whether stage 4 resumes the pending application, or the application has
    77	  to be posed again (restart cost, or intervention if done by hand).
    78	- P08c: what happens to the application at stage 3. Is it failed, held, or
    79	  resumed with the partial definition? Can stage 4 still reach it?
problems/09-concurrent-goals.md
     1	# P09. Concurrent goals
     2	
     3	**Probes:** whether a system can run computations that do not depend on
     4	each other without one blocking the other, and whether computations can
     5	work together through information they share. A system may support one and
     6	not the other.
     7	
     8	All computations run in one instance of the system (`PROTOCOL.md`). loop
     9	is as in P04. rev and conc are as in P03.
    10	
    11	## P09a. Independent computations
    12	
    13	Two separate computations, each started with `spawn` (`PROTOCOL.md`) in the
    14	same running system, not conjoined, sharing no unknowns:
    15	
    16	- A: loop(x)
    17	- B: rev(⟨1, 2, 3⟩, b)
    18	
    19	Valid outcomes: none for A; b = ⟨3, 2, 1⟩ for B. Posed in both orders, A
    20	then B and B then A. Each computation has its own budget.
    21	
    22	## P09b. Isolation from failure
    23	
    24	Two separate computations:
    25	
    26	- A: x = 1 and x = 2
    27	- B: y = 3
    28	
    29	Valid outcomes: none for A; y = 3 for B.
    30	
    31	## P09c. Communication through a shared unknown
    32	
    33	gen(n, s): s = ⟨n, n+1, …, 5⟩. It is defined, in this order, by:
    34	
    35	1. gen(n, ⟨⟩) if n > 5
    36	2. gen(n, ⟨n⟩ ++ rest) if n ≤ 5 and gen(n + 1, rest)
    37	
    38	sum3(s, total): total is the sum of the first three elements of s. It is
    39	defined by:
    40	
    41	1. sum3(⟨p, q, r⟩ ++ m, total) if p + q + r = total, for every sequence m
    42	
    43	One computation: gen(1, s) and sum3(s, total), in that order. Valid
    44	outcome: total = 6, s = ⟨1, 2, 3, 4, 5⟩.
    45	
    46	## P09d. An unbounded producer
    47	
    48	genu(n, s) is defined by one case:
    49	
    50	1. genu(n, ⟨n⟩ ++ rest) if genu(n + 1, rest)
    51	
    52	One computation: genu(1, s) and sum3(s, total), in that order. It is run
    53	once and classified twice, under each of two readings:
    54	
    55	- **P09d1, finite:** sequences are finite, and genu is read as the least
    56	  relation. It has no instances, so the conjunction has no solution. Valid
    57	  outcome: none. A system that reports total = 6 as a finished outcome is
    58	  *unsound*. One that has bound total to 6 while the producer is still
    59	  running, and reports nothing finished, is *none reported* with *budget*,
    60	  and the binding is recorded as an observation.
    61	- **P09d2, stream:** sequences may be infinite, and genu is read as the
    62	  greatest relation, so s = ⟨1, 2, 3, …⟩. Valid outcome: total = 6, while
    63	  the producer goes on. A system that computes total = 6 is *complete* for
    64	  total, whether or not the producer ever stops. The producer's state is
    65	  recorded separately: *budget*, *suspended*, or demand-driven and idle.
    66	
    67	## P09e. Mutual waiting
    68	
    69	One computation of two parts, each needing a value only the other can give:
    70	x = y + 1 and y = x − 1. Valid outcomes: every integer pair with
    71	x = y + 1.
    72	
    73	## Observations
    74	
    75	- P09a: whether B's outcome is reported while A runs, and whether the
    76	  order in which they are posed matters. If the system can only run them
    77	  one after another, record that.
    78	- P09b: whether A's failure affects B.
    79	- P09c and P09d: whether the consumer acts on s before the producer has
    80	  finished, from a trace if the system gives one, and in what order the two
    81	  parts take their steps.
    82	- P09e: whether the system reports a residual, suspends, enumerates, or
    83	  reports a deadlock, and whether that state can be told apart from
    84	  completion.

