    {
      "problem": "P14",
      "file": "problems/14-message-stream.md",
     1	# Specification
     2	
     3	*Draft 0.7, 27 November 2026. Not frozen; the freeze candidate. Draft 0.1 was reviewed
     4	independently; the findings and the response to each are in
     5	[`reviews/`](reviews). Draft 0.3 added the decisions taken on draft 0.2
     6	(`reviews/decisions-0.2.md`). Draft 0.4 answered the second review, of
     7	draft 0.3 (`reviews/review-2.md`). Draft 0.5 applied the decisions on
     8	draft 0.4 (`reviews/decisions-0.4.md`). Draft 0.6 answers the third review
     9	(`reviews/review-3.md`). See `PROTOCOL.md` for how the specification is
    10	frozen.*
    11	
    12	## The question
    13	
    14	**How do different computational models represent, retain and resolve
    15	partially specified computations?**
    16	
    17	A computation is *partially specified* when the information it needs has not
    18	all arrived. Some of its unknowns have no value yet, some of its relations
    19	have no definition yet, or some of its alternatives cannot yet be told
    20	apart. This study poses the same partially specified problems to several
    21	systems and records what each does with them.
    22	
    23	The results are meant to map trade-offs along six dimensions of design, not
    24	to rank systems:
    25	
    26	| dimension | what it covers | problems that bear most on it |
    27	|---|---|---|
    28	| **information** | how incomplete knowledge is represented | P01, P02, P06, P07, P16 |
    29	| **execution** | how computations become runnable: order, scheduling, suspension, resumption | P02, P04, P08, P13 |
    30	| **state** | how information changes over time | P10, P14 |
    31	| **search** | how alternatives are explored and retained | P03, P04, P05, P12 |
    32	| **coordination** | how computations communicate and cooperate | P09, P14, P15, P16 |
    33	| **commitment** | how tentative results become final, and irreversible actions | P11, P15 |
    34	
    35	**Resources** cut across all six: retained state, recomputation, control
    36	code, supporting code and intervention. Each metric in `METRICS.md` names
    37	the dimension it measures, or resources.
    38	
    39	## What this specification does not assume
    40	
    41	None of the following is taken as true. Each is a question the results may
    42	bear on:
    43	
    44	- that declarative computation is better than imperative computation;
    45	- that a fair search is better than a depth-first one;
    46	- that suspending a computation is better than restarting it;
    47	- that keeping alternatives alive is better than backtracking;
    48	- that less explicit control code makes a better programming model;
    49	- that information only ever accumulates. Some problems replace or
    50	  retract information (P10).
    51	
    52	Each design may have advantages under different limits of memory, time or
    53	implementation effort.
    54	
    55	## The problems
    56	
    57	| | problem | what it probes |
    58	|---|---|---|
    59	| P01 | [unknown equality](problems/01-unknown-equality.md) | how a relation between unknowns is represented |
    60	| P02 | [late information](problems/02-late-information.md) | whether a computation resumes when information arrives |
    61	| P03 | [reversible relations](problems/03-reversible-relations.md) | whether one definition serves several directions |
    62	| P04 | [unbounded alternative](problems/04-unbounded-alternative.md) | whether an endless alternative hides a finite one, in either order |
    63	| P05 | [late contradiction](problems/05-late-contradiction.md) | how alternatives are kept and dropped as constraints arrive |
    64	| P06 | [negation](problems/06-negation.md) | whether negation is sound while its unknowns are unknown |
    65	| P07 | [simultaneous constraints](problems/07-simultaneous-constraints.md) | which sets of equations are solved, and how |
    66	| P08 | [late definition](problems/08-late-definition.md) | what an application of a relation not yet defined means and does |
    67	| P09 | [concurrent goals](problems/09-concurrent-goals.md) | independent computations, and computations that share information |
    68	| P10 | [state replacement](problems/10-state-replacement.md) | values that change, relations that keep holding, information withdrawn |
    69	| P11 | [irreversible effects](problems/11-irreversible-effects.md) | whether external actions are tied to committed outcomes |
    70	| P12 | [large alternative space](problems/12-large-alternative-space.md) | the time and store of each search strategy |
    71	| P13 | [deterministic scale](problems/13-deterministic-scale.md) | how bookkeeping grows with size, apart from search |
    72	| P14 | [message stream](problems/14-message-stream.md) | a persistent process with state, answering between messages |
    73	| P15 | [merge and committed choice](problems/15-merge-and-committed-choice.md) | interleaving, fairness and arbitration of a resource |
    74	| P16 | [shared information at scale](problems/16-shared-information.md) | propagation, contributors, localised updates and contradiction in a large structure of relations |
    75	
    76	The problems are stated in mathematical notation only. A definition's
    77	numbered cases describe a relation; they are not a program, and a program
    78	may express them in whatever form its language uses.
    79	
    80	## How a problem is stated
    81	
    82	Each problem file gives:
    83	
    84	1. **Vocabulary.** The relations, with a definition by cases for every
    85	   relation the problem uses that is not primitive.
    86	2. **Stages.** The information initially available (stage 1), and each
    87	   later piece of information, in the order it arrives. A problem of one
    88	   stage is a query.
    89	3. **Presentation order.** Where a definition or a conjunction has parts,
    90	   the order in which they are written. Some systems' behaviour depends on
    91	   it, so it is part of the problem. A program that changes it is
    92	   *reformulated* (`METRICS.md`).
    93	4. **Valid outcomes.** After every stage, the set of solutions that the
    94	   information so far admits, under the semantics below and any reading the
    95	   problem names.
    96	5. **Observations.** What to record, beyond the common observations below.
    97	
    98	## Conventions
    99	
   100	### Values and names
   101	
   102	- **Values** are integers, atoms and finite sequences of values.
   103	- **Atoms** are written in small capitals, as ᴀ, ʙʀᴏᴡɴ, ᴄ101. In plain
   104	  text they may be written with a leading quote: 'a, 'brown, 'c101.
   105	- ⟨⟩ is the empty sequence, ⟨1,2⟩ a sequence of two, and a ++ b the
   106	  sequence a followed by b. ++ is defined only on sequences.
   107	- **Unknowns** are written in italic lower case where the format allows,
   108	  and are never the same letters as an atom or a relation in the same
   109	  problem. An unknown ranges over all values unless its problem says
   110	  otherwise. Where a relation's case says "for every sequence b", b ranges
   111	  over sequences only.
   112	- **Relations** are written in lower case words: conc, rev, mem.
   113	
   114	### Definitions
   115	
   116	- A definition is a list of numbered cases. Each is a fact or an
   117	  implication "r(…) if …", whose conjuncts are joined by *and* in the order
   118	  written.
   119	- A definition with no negation is read as the **least relation**
   120	  satisfying its cases.
   121	- A definition with negation is read under the **stratified** (perfect
   122	  model) semantics. Negation is applied only to relations defined at a lower
   123	  stratum, as every definition in this suite does.
   124	- Where a problem says a relation is given by facts in a **closed world**,
   125	  only the stated facts are true.
   126	- Some problems also name a second reading, such as a stream reading over
   127	  infinite sequences. There, the problem gives valid outcomes under each
   128	  reading, and a result is classified under each separately.
   129	
   130	### Arithmetic and comparison
   131	
   132	The primitive vocabulary is +, −, · on integers, written as relations
   133	(a + b = c holds of three integers; any place may be unknown), and the
   134	comparisons =, ≠, <, ≤, >, ≥. Comparisons other than = and ≠ hold only of
   135	integers. A compound expression such as x + y + z = 12 abbreviates a
   136	conjunction of primitive relations with fresh unknowns. Writing it either
   137	way, compound or decomposed, is a transcription of the problem
   138	(`METRICS.md`, class P).
   139	
   140	## Stages and monotonicity
   141	
   142	A stage adds information to what came before it, unless the problem marks it
   143	as **replacing** (a named quantity takes a new value) or **retracting**
   144	(information given earlier is withdrawn), as P10 does. Two monotonicities
   145	are distinguished:
   146	
   147	- **Information monotonicity.** The stated information only grows. This
   148	  holds in every problem except those that say they replace or retract.
   149	- **Conclusion monotonicity.** Every conclusion a system reports stays
   150	  valid once later information arrives. This does not follow from the first.
   151	  Under a closed world, adding a definition or a fact can make a conclusion
   152	  that was valid (no solutions; a negation true) invalid at the next stage.
   153	
   154	The valid outcomes after stage k are always computed from the whole of the
   155	information of stages 1 to k, under the problem's readings. Where they
   156	contradict a valid outcome of an earlier stage, the problem file says so.
   157	Each result records every reported conclusion that a later stage withdrew,
   158	and how the system showed the withdrawal, if it did.
   159	
   160	## Outcomes
   161	
   162	Each stage of each run is classified on three axes, recorded separately,
   163	and on a fourth, effects, in P11 only. A residual representation also
   164	records its simplification (below).
   165	
   166	Separately from the axes, **expressibility** is recorded once per
   167	configuration and sub-problem (for a relation used in several directions,
   168	once per direction), by one rule for every system:
   169	
   170	| expressibility | meaning | the program of record |
   171	|---|---|---|
   172	| **expressible** | the problem's definitions can be transcribed | the transcription |
   173	| **expressible with reformulation** | it cannot be transcribed, but a reformulation keeps the relation's meaning on every input the problem and its hidden variants use | the reformulation, with its R tokens counted |
   174	| **not expressible** | only a reformulation that changes the meaning (dropping a guard the inputs depend on, for instance), or supporting code, can be written | none. Any such reformulation is run and reported as a named variant |
   175	
   176	Both the classification and any named variant are always reported. That a
   177	reformulation was needed is itself a result.
   178	
   179	### 1. Semantic result
   180	
   181	Judged on the set of solutions the report denotes, compared with the valid
   182	outcomes.
   183	
   184	| class | meaning |
   185	|---|---|
   186	| **complete** | the report denotes exactly the valid outcomes. Whether an empty set was reported as empty is recorded by simplification (*inconsistency undetected*) and by answer completeness |
   187	| **partial** | the report denotes a nonempty proper subset of the valid outcomes. This includes a representation that drops solutions, such as a solver that restricts an unknown to integers when the problem lets it range wider |
   188	| **unsound** | the report denotes an outcome that is not valid, or reports that there are none where valid outcomes exist |
   189	| **undetermined** | the report consists of conditions whose equivalence to the valid outcomes the classifiers cannot establish |
   190	| **none reported** | nothing was reported |
   191	
   192	A system that reports one answer of several, for a query that asks for one
   193	(*any one* queries, in later problems), is *complete* for that query.
   194	
   195	**Correctness and simplification are kept apart.** A residual that is
   196	equivalent to the valid outcomes is *complete*, however unreduced. How far
   197	the system reduced it is recorded separately, as **simplification**:
   198	
   199	| simplification | meaning |
   200	|---|---|
   201	| **reduced** | no smaller equivalent representation exists |
   202	| **reducible** | a smaller equivalent representation exists, which the system did not reach |
   203	| **inconsistency undetected** | the conditions denote the empty set, and the system has not reported that there are none |
   204	
   205	One representation is smaller than another if it has fewer relations left
   206	to decide, or the same number with fewer occurrences of unknowns. So:
   207	
   208	- P02, stage 1: u + v = 16, held, is *complete* / *residual* / *reduced*.
   209	- P07c: x + y = 10, held, is *complete* / *residual* / *reduced*.
   210	- P07b: x + y = 10 and x − y = 4, held, is *complete* / *residual* /
   211	  *reducible*, since x = 7, y = 3 is smaller.
   212	- P07f: x + y = 10 and x + y = 11, held, is *complete* / *residual* /
   213	  *inconsistency undetected*.
   214	
   215	So a system that does nothing with a set of relations it cannot solve is
   216	not marked wrong. Its simplification shows what it left undone, and is
   217	compared alongside the semantic result.
   218	
   219	A binding that the system holds is **reported** if the harness's
   220	`observe()` can read it at the end of the stage, including at the moment
   221	a budget runs out, whether or not the system's mechanism for answers
   222	would deliver it (P09d2, P15b). The one exception is P09d1, whose finite
   223	reading has no solution: there, a binding held while the producer still
   224	runs is recorded as an observation, and the result is *none reported*.
   225	
   226	### 2. Representation
   227	
   228	| class | meaning |
   229	|---|---|
   230	| **explicit** | each solution is given as values |
   231	| **solved form** | each unknown is either bound to a term built from values, unknowns, ⟨⟩ and ++, or free, or identified with another unknown, and no relation remains to be decided |
   232	| **residual** | some relations remain to be decided. The residual conditions are recorded, with their size beside the size of the input |
   233	| **cyclic** | a term that is not finite (a rational tree), presented as such |
   234	
   235	Whether a residual is equivalent to the valid outcomes is checked by the
   236	classifiers (`PROTOCOL.md`, Roles), by hand or by a published checking
   237	script, with the reasoning recorded. Sizes of representations are measured
   238	on the primitive decomposition (`Conventions`).
   239	
   240	Where a program reports a stage in two forms (the relations it holds, and
   241	the solutions it derived from them, for instance), the representation is
   242	classified on the more explicit form that is valid, and the other is
   243	recorded as an observation. A form derived by reformulation keeps its R
   244	tokens. *(Clarified after the held-out check.)*
   245	
   246	A residual counts as reported only if it is state that the system or the
   247	program holds and acts on at a later stage, read by `observe()`. Printing
   248	the posed information back is not a report. Beside simplification, a flag
   249	records a residual **identical to the posed information**, so that a
   250	system that held the relations and did nothing yet can be told apart from
   251	one that transformed them.
   252	
   253	### 3. Execution state
   254	
   255	| class | meaning |
   256	|---|---|
   257	| **finished** | the system has stopped and holds no more work for this stage |
   258	| **alternatives pending** | the system has stopped after an answer, and holds alternatives that a request for more answers would resume |
   259	| **suspended** | the system has stopped, and holds work that later information may resume |
   260	| **budget** | the step budget ran out (`PROTOCOL.md`). Nontermination is presumed, not proved |
   261	| **timeout** | the harness's safety limit on time ran out before the budget did. The run is rerun once with the limit raised (`PROTOCOL.md`). Answers reported before the limit are classified on the semantic axis |
   262	| **error** | the system stopped with an error, recorded verbatim |
   263	
   264	A run may be both *alternatives pending* and *suspended*. Both are
   265	recorded.
   266	
   267	### 4. Effects
   268	
   269	Only for problems with external effects (P11), a fourth axis
   270	records whether the effects performed match the outcome committed to. P11
   271	defines it.
   272	
   273	## Observations common to every problem
   274	
   275	After each stage, record:
   276	
   277	1. the outcomes reported, verbatim, collected as `PROTOCOL.md` says;
   278	2. the three classifications above;
   279	3. how the unknowns that remain unknown are represented;
   280	4. the alternatives the system holds, and whether they are all live at once
   281	   or kept for backtracking, in the unit its systems file defines;
   282	5. what the system holds as suspended or waiting work, in its own terms,
   283	   and how many items;
   284	6. the metrics of `METRICS.md` that apply to a stage;
   285	7. any conclusion reported at an earlier stage that this stage withdrew;
   286	8. any manual action needed to pose the stage.
   287	
   288	## Disclosure: where the problems came from
   289	
   290	P01–P08 were first run, in other forms, on a reconstruction of Absys
   291	(1967–68), and most were chosen because Absys did something notable with
   292	them. The reconstruction itself was developed while those experiments ran,
   293	and some of its decisions were taken because of them
   294	(`systems/absys/README.md`). The suite may therefore favour systems that
   295	resemble Absys. Disclosure does not remove that, so:
   296	
   297	- P07 includes cases that Absys is known or expected to handle badly.
   298	- P10–P16 were added after review: state replacement, effects, large
   299	  search spaces, scale, persistent processes, arbitration, and shared
   300	  information at scale. These are
   301	  problems where imperative, backtracking and concurrent designs are
   302	  expected to do well, and Absys may do badly or not at all.
   303	- The first comparison includes two imperative baselines (Python and
   304	  ALGOL 60), so that the suite is not only a comparison of declarative
   305	  systems.
   306	- Retained state and recomputation are measured as costs. Each system's
   307	  store is measured in its own units, which are not comparable. What is
   308	  compared across systems is the **problem-defined quantities**, such as
   309	  the alternatives a problem's own terms say are held, and the **growth
   310	  exponent** of each system's store and steps as a problem's size grows
   311	  (`METRICS.md`).
     1	# Protocol
     2	
     3	*Draft 0.7.*
     4	
     5	## Phases
     6	
     7	1. **Draft.** `SPECIFICATION.md`, `METRICS.md`, this file and `problems/`
     8	   are written. No program for the study is written, apart from the
     9	   calibration programs (`METRICS.md`, Calibration), which are development
    10	   examples and never results.
    11	   Measurement may be qualified at this phase: establishing what each
    12	   system can report, and adding instrumentation that changes no result,
    13	   under the conditions in "Instrumentation" below.
    14	2. **Independent review.** A reviewer who has not written the draft reads
    15	   it for ambiguity, for errors in the valid outcomes, for language bias,
    16	   and for problems missing from the suite. Findings and responses are kept
    17	   in `reviews/`. *(Draft 0.1 had one review, answered in draft 0.2. Draft
    18	   0.3 had a second, by a fresh reviewer, answered in draft 0.4. Draft 0.5
    19	   had a third, answered in draft 0.6. Draft 0.7 is the freeze candidate.)*
    20	3. **Freeze.** The specification is tagged `spec-1.0`, together with:
    21	   - the pinned version or commit of every system in the first comparison;
    22	   - each systems file's cores, flags and units;
    23	   - the generic harness interface below.
    24	
    25	   From then on, these change only by amendment.
    26	4. **Harnesses.** One harness per system implements the interface, and is
    27	   published before any program is run.
    28	5. **Implementation.** Programs are written for each configuration from the
    29	   problem files, one per configuration and problem, fixed before any of
    30	   them is run.
    31	6. **Results.** Each program is run and classified, and the results are
    32	   recorded in `results/`.
    33	7. **Comparison.** Only after results exist for at least two systems on
    34	   every problem.
    35	
    36	No system is implemented before the freeze. The benchmark is not changed
    37	afterwards to suit any system, including PLANNER and the systems after it.
    38	
    39	## Amendments
    40	
    41	An amendment is a numbered, dated change to a frozen file, with its reason.
    42	It applies to every system. Results obtained before it are kept, marked
    43	with the specification version they were obtained under, and rerun under
    44	the new version.
    45	
    46	A problem proposed after the freeze does not join the frozen suite. It goes
    47	into a separate, dated supplementary suite, reported apart from the frozen
    48	one, with the date it was proposed and the results that existed then.
    49	
    50	## Roles
    51	
    52	| role | does | has access to |
    53	|---|---|---|
    54	| **specification author** | writes the problems and the protocol | the public repository |
    55	| **variant custodian** | writes the hidden variants and their valid outcomes | the specification, and the private variants |
    56	| **implementation author** | writes the programs | the public repository only |
    57	| **harness author** | writes each system's harness (phase 4) | the public repository |
    58	| **classifier A** | classes the programs' constructs, and the outcomes of every run on all axes, checking residual equivalence | the specification, the metrics, the programs, their outputs and the valid outcomes |
    59	| **classifier B** | does both independently | the same, without A's classes |
    60	| **verifier** | runs the programs on the hidden variants | the programs, the variants and their outcomes |
    61	
    62	Each role is a separate agent or person, with its own context, and its
    63	provenance is recorded. The two classifiers are of different origin (for
    64	instance Claude and Codex), and neither wrote the program it classes.
    65	Their disagreements, on tokens and on outcomes, are recorded, and a
    66	disputed outcome is reported under both classes.
    67	
    68	**Hidden variants** are made inaccessible, not merely placed where an
    69	implementation author is told not to look. The custodian writes them
    70	before the freeze and encrypts them with a key the project's owner holds
    71	but does not use. The plaintext begins with a random nonce of at least 128
    72	bits, so that a small space of constants cannot be recovered from its
    73	hash. Only the ciphertext and the SHA-256 hash of the plaintext are
    74	committed. The key is given to the verifier only after every primary
    75	program and its results are committed. The verifier publishes the
    76	plaintext, and anyone can check it against the hash. Each later wave of
    77	systems (PLANNER onwards) has its own sealed set, made before that wave's
    78	programs are written, since revealing a set publishes it.
    79	
    80	## Evidence classification
    81	
    82	Every implementation is given one or more of these classes:
    83	
    84	| class | meaning |
    85	|---|---|
    86	| **original** | a surviving historical implementation, on the hardware or software it was written for |
    87	| **rehosted** | a surviving historical implementation, running in a modern environment (an emulator, a port of the original source) |
    88	| **reconstructed** | a modern implementation derived from historical documents, where no implementation survives |
    89	| **modern** | a contemporary implementation of the language |
    90	| **extended** | mechanisms supplied for the experiment, beyond the implementation's own |
    91	
    92	Two qualifiers may be attached to a class:
    93	
    94	- *with external mechanisms*: libraries or run-time features outside the
    95	  configuration's core are used (`METRICS.md`);
    96	- *alternative reading*: a reconstruction run with a decision taken the
    97	  other way, where its sources conflict.
    98	
    99	Results are always reported with the class. No result from a modern or
   100	extended configuration is attributed to the historical language. Where a
   101	language was described more fully than it was implemented (PLANNER and
   102	Micro-Planner, for instance), the described language and the implemented
   103	subset are separate systems.
   104	
   105	## Configurations
   106	
   107	A configuration is one system with a stated set of mechanisms. Results are
   108	kept separate per configuration, so that a capability introduced later is
   109	not attributed to the earlier model. Each systems file lists, per
   110	configuration:
   111	
   112	- its core, built-in by built-in;
   113	- its flags;
   114	- the unit of steps, of an alternative and of store, and how each is
   115	  measured;
   116	- what "the same computation" means. That is, whether a stage can be
   117	  received into a computation already running, and how.
   118	
   119	A reconstruction is pinned at a commit when the specification is frozen.
   120	Any later change to it, for whatever reason, makes a new configuration,
   121	reported separately, and never replaces the pinned one.
   122	
   123	## Instrumentation
   124	
   125	A measurement added to a system must not change what it computes. For a
   126	reconstruction:
   127	
   128	- the unmodified commit is kept as the baseline;
   129	- instrumentation is made on a separate branch, and recorded as its own
   130	  commit;
   131	- it may not change scheduling, search, equality, suspension or any other
   132	  rule of the machine;
   133	- the instrumented build, with measurement on and off, must give output
   134	  identical to the baseline's, byte for byte, on every example and in every
   135	  output mode, including full traces. The check and its result are
   136	  recorded.
   137	
   138	## The harness interface
   139	
   140	Every harness provides exactly these operations, and nothing that orders,
   141	chooses or re-poses goals on its own:
   142	
   143	- **pose(stage)**: give the system the information of one stage, into the
   144	  same computation if the configuration allows it, or else by reposing.
   145	  A reposed run replays **every** stage 1 to k in order, replacements and
   146	  withdrawals included, and the program must carry them out. The harness
   147	  never works out the net information itself;
   148	- **spawn(computation)**: start a separate computation in the same
   149	  instance (P09), returning at once where the configuration can run
   150	  computations side by side. In a configuration that cannot, the first
   151	  runs to its end or its budget, and then the next;
   152	- **answers(cap)**: collect the answers the system reports, through its
   153	  standard mechanism for enumerating them, until it is exhausted, the
   154	  budget runs out, or `cap` answers have been collected. `cap` is 1 for an
   155	  *any one* query and 20 otherwise, as each problem states. The execution
   156	  state is classified after the call;
   157	- **observe()**: record the state the common observations need, including
   158	  bindings the system holds (`SPECIFICATION.md`, "reported");
   159	- **print()**.
   160	
   161	Each system's systems file says what its standard mechanism for answers
   162	is. For Python and ALGOL 60, it is the values the program prints.
   163	
   164	**Observing must not consume the computation.** In a system where
   165	collecting answers would end or alter the computation that the next stage
   166	extends (exhausting a backtracking search, for instance), the observation
   167	of stage k is taken on a separate run of stages 1 to k. The incremental
   168	run is left as it was.
   169	
   170	**Commitments are preserved when a run is reposed.** When a commit stage
   171	has selected an outcome v (P11), a reposed run replays it as the
   172	information x = v. It does not ask the system to select again, which could
   173	commit to a different outcome.
   174	
   175	**External effects** (P11) are written to one log per problem, never reset,
   176	across every run the harness makes for classification, reposed runs
   177	included. Separate observation runs write to a separate log, which is not
   178	judged. Entries that are duplicated only because a run was reposed are
   179	counted separately, as the cost of reposing an irreversible effect.
   180	
   181	Concurrency used to run computations (threads, processes) is a mechanism of
   182	the configuration, listed in its systems file. A harness may not run
   183	computations concurrently itself. In P09, all computations run in one
   184	instance of the system, sharing one database, never in separate operating
   185	system processes.
   186	
   187	## Writing a program
   188	
   189	1. Write it from the problem file. Programs written for this study's
   190	   precursors (the absys experiments E01–E09) exist, and their author may
   191	   have seen them; each program says whether its author had.
   192	2. Use the idioms of the system's period where they are known. Cite the
   193	   source of each idiom: a manual, a paper or a published program.
   194	3. Transcribe the definitions given. Anything else is reformulation, and
   195	   counts as R.
   196	4. Where several idiomatic programs are possible, choose one per
   197	   configuration and problem before any is run, or run and report every
   198	   variant. In either case, one is designated the **primary** variant
   199	   before any is run, and stands for the configuration.
   200	5. **No anticipation.** The code that handles stage k may not depend on the
   201	   content of any later stage.
   202	6. **Genericity.** A program must stay correct when the constants in a
   203	   problem's stages are changed. It keeps them in one marked place, or reads
   204	   them as input, so that the verifier can substitute them. Each problem
   205	   has hidden variants with other constants, held by someone who does not
   206	   write programs. Every program is run on them after its results are
   207	   recorded. The custodian works out each variant's valid outcomes
   208	   independently, stage by stage, as the problem files give them for the
   209	   original constants. A variant's outcomes need not have the same classes
   210	   as the original's: other constants can make a finite outcome infinite,
   211	   or a consistent stage inconsistent. A program's run on a variant is
   212	   classified against **that variant's** valid outcomes, on the semantic and
   213	   effects axes, exactly as a primary run is. A program **fails** a variant
   214	   where that classification differs from its classification on the
   215	   original constants, each against its own valid outcomes: a program
   216	   complete on the original and unsound on the variant fails it. It is
   217	   recorded as such, since it encodes a conclusion rather than computing
   218	   it. The custodian records, for each variant, which of the original's
   219	   outcome classes it was built to keep, if any.
   220	7. A program may not be revised after its results are seen, except to fix a
   221	   transcription error: a place where the program departs from the text of
   222	   the problem file. Each fix is recorded as a diff, with the reason. A
   223	   program changed for any other reason is a separate, named variant, and
   224	   is reported as such.
   225	8. Record the author of each program, human or agent.
   226	
   227	## Running
   228	
   229	- Pin the version of every system, and record its source and commit.
   230	- **Budget.** 10⁶ steps in the system's own unit, per stage and per
   231	  computation. In P09, each separate computation has its own budget and its
   232	  own classification. A reposed run of stages 1 to k has a budget of its
   233	  measured restart cost plus 10⁶.
   234	- **The budget is relative to each system.** 10⁶ Absys steps, SWI-Prolog
   235	  inferences and Python instructions are different amounts of work. So
   236	  *budget* is a classification within a system, and is not compared
   237	  across systems. Each system records, as a calibration figure, its steps
   238	  for P13b at n = 10³, or, where P13b is not expressible, for P03e.
   239	- **After budget.** If stage k ends in *budget* and the configuration can
   240	  keep its computation and continue it, stage k+1 is posed into that same
   241	  computation, with a new budget. Whether it can is recorded, since
   242	  continuing past an exhausted budget is itself a capability. Stages 1 to
   243	  k+1 are reposed only when the configuration cannot continue, and the
   244	  reposing counts under restart cost.
   245	- **After error.** Stage k+1 is posed by reposing stages 1 to k+1, counted
   246	  under restart cost.
   247	- **Safety limit.** A limit of 60 seconds per stage protects the harness.
   248	  It is not a measurement. A stage it stops is rerun once, with the limit
   249	  raised tenfold. If the rerun also stops, *timeout* is final for that
   250	  stage. Both runs are recorded. Answers reported before the limit stopped
   251	  the run are kept and classified on the semantic axis; only the execution
   252	  state is *timeout*.
   253	
   254	## Modes of running
   255	
   256	Each program is run in up to three modes:
   257	
   258	| mode | what it establishes | instrumentation |
   259	|---|---|---|
   260	| **behaviour** | the outcomes, the three axes and effects | none beyond the harness |
   261	| **cost** | steps, answer latency, problem-defined operation counts | step counting only |
   262	| **storage** | store, retained alternatives, suspended state | the systems file's storage measurement |
   263	
   264	- The semantic result and execution state are taken from the behaviour run.
   265	- Where two modes can be compared, their semantic results must agree, and
   266	  any disagreement is reported as a fault of the instrumentation.
   267	- A sampled measurement is never taken from the run that gives the step
   268	  count or the classification, because sampling adds steps of its own.
   269	- A run that hits the safety limit only because it is instrumented (in
   270	  cost or storage mode, when the behaviour run finished) is not evidence
   271	  that the model cannot complete the problem. It is recorded as a limit of
   272	  the measurement.
   273	- Run in CI where the system allows it, and compare with recorded output.
   274	
   275	## Recording a result
   276	
   277	Each result is a file `results/<system>/<configuration>/<problem>.md` that
   278	gives:
   279	
   280	- the specification version, the system version or commit, the
   281	  configuration and the evidence class;
   282	- the program, or a link to it at a commit, and whether its author had seen
   283	  a precursor;
   284	- for each stage, the common observations of `SPECIFICATION.md` and the
   285	  problem's own;
   286	- the token counts by class, per classifier, with the disputed tokens;
   287	- the configuration's expressibility for the problem;
   288	- the supporting code (S) used, by mechanism. An *extended* result is named
   289	  by its mechanism. Every configuration's S mechanisms are listed, with
   290	  their size, at phase 4, before any program is run;
   291	- whether the program passed its hidden variants.
   292	
   293	## Guarding against bias
   294	
   295	- The reasons for choosing a problem, and the history of each system's
   296	  implementation, are disclosed.
   297	- Every review is asked for problems that favour designs other than those
   298	  the suite came from.
   299	- Results are reported for every system on every problem, including
   300	  "not expressible".
   301	- Both classifiers' token classes are kept and reported for every result,
   302	  throughout the study, never only an adjudicated set. A comparative
   303	  conclusion that would change if one classifier's classes were used in
   304	  place of the other's is reported as **classification-sensitive**, with
   305	  both figures.
   306	- No ranking is computed. A summary may say where a system needed control,
   307	  supporting code or intervention that another did not, and what it held in
   308	  store to avoid them.
   309	- Interpretation, including any connection to later work, is kept out of
   310	  `results/` and in a separate document written after the comparison.

