   136	## Q9. "One identical stub for every system"
   137	
   138	P11 says the harness provides L and emit "as one identical stub for every
   139	system". The four systems cannot run the same code.
   140	
   141	**Implemented:** the stub is identical in behaviour and in its log format
   142	(`README.md`, section 6): append m, rendered as the system renders it,
   143	with its run, stage, attempt and replay context; never fail, defer,
   144	buffer past the stage, deduplicate or read. Each system harness binds it
   145	into its language and lists that binding's tokens as H.
   146	
   147	## Q10. P13's "larger values of n are not run after it"
   148	
   149	P13 says a run stopped by either limit is recorded and the larger values
   150	of n are not run after it. A run of the harness is one instance (one n),
   151	so `run` cannot apply it. P12 and P16 take P13's *limits* ("as in P13")
   152	but do not repeat this rule.
   153	
   154	**Implemented:** the rule is applied by whoever runs the batch, across
   155	runs, for P13 only (`stages.json`: `stop_larger_n_after_limit` on P13
   156	alone), per configuration, task and mode, and an n not run is recorded
   157	as not run. P12 and P16 run every n. *Why:* the text states the rule
   158	under P13's own budget section; extending it is an amendment.
   159	
   160	## Q11. No rerun in P12, P13 and P16
   161	
   162	`PROTOCOL.md` reruns a stopped stage once with the limit raised tenfold;
   163	P12, P13 and P16 fix their limit of 600 s and say it is not raised.
   164	
   165	**Implemented:** in those three, the first stop is final
   166	(`safety_limit.action = "fixed-limit-timeout"`, state *timeout*); there
   167	is no second attempt (`rerun_limit_s: null`). The problem's own text is
   168	the more specific.
